<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konark Sun Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Konark Sun Temple is a 13th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to the sun god Surya at Konark on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built around 1250 by the Eastern Ganga king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i">Narasimhadeva I</a>. It is designed as a giant stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> with twenty-four carved wheels drawn by seven horses. Its main tower has collapsed, but the surviving hall was declared a World Heritage Site in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konark-sun-temple.jpg" alt="Konark Sun Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Subham9423 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konarka_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
